summary: The repository's history as a tree of labels — a unit of work per row (a command, a script's run, the watcher's flushes folded), opening down to single operations, with badges saying what each is.

Open it with `panel:open here log` (<<key "panel:open here log" "t o">>). One row per
''unit of work'', newest first by its newest operation: for what you wrote, the outermost
label it was written in — a command, a script's run, a context you opened ([[Labels and contexts]]) —; for the watcher, one flush. Consecutive flushes of the watcher fold into a
single row (`N flushes of the watcher`), or they would drown the rest. Going into a row
lists the labels nested in it and its own operations, newest first, down to single
operations: a run onto its answers, an answer onto its commands, a command onto what it
wrote — and an operation onto what it changed. The list shows one level at a time, as the [[treeref panel]] does; the
breadcrumb above it says where it is, each step a way back.

A label row shows its number, name and description, the time of its newest operation,
how many operations of the window are under it, and ''status'' badges — what decides
whether undoing it makes sense, computed over everything under it:

* `HEAD` — the newest operation is under it;
* `watcher` — the daemon wrote it for the filesystem ([[Operations and their origin]]); the row is dimmed, and `log:undo` skips it;
* `undo of #N` — it reverts what label `#N` wrote;
* `undone by #N` (or `partly undone`) — a later revert still standing took it back; a row fully undone is struck through, and reverting it again says so first.

!! Moving around

* <<key "cursor:next self" "↓ or j">> / <<key "cursor:prev self" "↑ or k">>, <<key "cursor:first self" Home>> / <<key "cursor:last self" End>>, <<key "cursor:find self select" f>> (a row on screen by label number or name, [[Jumping to an entry]]) — the selection, over every listed row.
* <<key "cursor:descend self" "Enter or →">>, or the row's `↘` button ([[Opening a row]]) — go into the row under the cursor: the list shows what it holds, the cursor on its first row; an operation shows what it changed. A revert aims at what is under the cursor — a label whole, what is nested in it included, or one operation —, or at the operation gone into.
* <<key "cursor:parent self" "Backspace or ←">>, or ''↑ up'', or a step of the breadcrumb — back out, the cursor on the row it came out of.
* <<key "log:toggle watcher" Shift+w>> — hide or show the watcher's work (shown by default). A display filter over what was fetched; the status line says how many units are hidden, and a selection inside one is dropped.
* ''Show older operations'' — the list fetches the newest thousand operations, so a repository with millions of them still opens; the button widens the window by as much again.
* <<key "view:refresh self" r>> — read the log again. The status line gives how many units of work and operations are listed, and the repository's totals when the window is only part of the log.

!! What an operation changed

Going into an operation shows it in place of the list: its type, field and `undo of op N`
badge, the metarecord it wrote with that metarecord's ''current'' path — clicking the uuid
selects it and shows it in the other slot — and a before/after table: one row per field
name, a name's values compared as a set (fields are a multi-map), rows marked added,
removed or changed. Fields the operation recorded without changing are hidden, with a
count, unless `log:toggle unchanged` (the ''Unchanged fields'' checkbox). A
`tree_ref` value is shown as the path it names. It is read again with the log, so a revert
shows at once.

This replaced the `log-detail` panel, which showed the same beside the log, following its
cursor: one panel, gone into like the rest of the tree, rather than two kept in step.

!! Acting

<<key "log:revert" Shift+r>>, <<key "log:revert op" Shift+o>>, <<key "log:revert label" Shift+v>>,
`log:prune` — and the global <<key "log:undo" Ctrl+z>> / <<key "log:redo" Ctrl+Shift+z>>,
also as toolbar buttons: [[Log commands in the GUI]].
